// Deviation from upstream: upstream swaps `react-day-picker` for
// `react-day-picker/persian` and loads the Vazirmatn Google Font via
// `next/font/google` for Arabic-script glyphs. Our Calendar sits directly
// on @zag-js/date-picker, which supports non-Gregorian calendars through
// `@internationalized/date`'s own `createCalendar` (the same helper
// upstream's MDX documents at
// /docs/components/base/calendar#persian--hijri--jalali-calendar) paired
// with a Persian locale tag — no react-day-picker calendar swap needed.
// The Vazirmatn font is a cosmetic Next.js font loader (no Marko/Vite
// equivalent wired here) and is intentionally omitted; the browser's
// default Arabic-script fallback renders the digits/month names instead.
//
// `value`/`min`/`max` are intentionally left unset: our Calendar's public
// `value` is a plain `{ year, month, day }` object that
// packages/shadcn/ui/calendar/calendar.marko's `toDateValue()` always
// wraps in a Gregorian `CalendarDate` (it has no calendar-system
// parameter), regardless of the `locale`/`createCalendar` passed here. A
// hardcoded `{ year: 1404, ... }` would therefore be silently
// reinterpreted as a Gregorian date deep inside `@internationalized/date`,
// producing a wrong/invalid selection instead of the intended Persian
// date — a real limitation of the component's value mapping, not
// something this demo can work around. Supporting a controlled initial
// selection for non-Gregorian locales would need calendar-system-aware
// value conversion in calendar.marko itself, which is out of scope here.
//
// `defaultFocusedValue` is different: it bypasses that plain-object
// mapping entirely (Calendar's Input only omits `value`/`min`/`max`, not
// `focusedValue`/`defaultFocusedValue`), so it takes a real Zag/
// @internationalized/date `DateValue` directly, calendar-system and all.
// It's required here, not just nice-to-have: without it the Zag
// date-picker machine falls back to the browser's wall-clock "today"
// (`getTodayDate`), which makes this demo's SSR markup diverge from its
// hydrated markup near midnight or across timezones — this page is
// exactly what the hydration-invariant suite scans. `toCalendar()`
// converts a fixed Gregorian date into the Persian calendar system so the
// pinned date renders correctly in Farsi digits/months, not
// (re)interpreted as a raw Gregorian date the way the `value` prop would.
import Calendar from "@marko-ui/shadcn/ui/calendar/calendar.marko";
import { CalendarDate, createCalendar as createCalendarInstance, toCalendar } from "@internationalized/date";

// `createCalendar` and `defaultFocusedValue` are BOTH computed inline
// (function calls in the tag body) rather than as `static const` values
// referenced by name: a `CalendarDate` built against a non-Gregorian
// calendar carries a `.calendar` field that is itself a class instance
// with methods — same class of value as a bare function reference — and
// neither survives Marko's SSR-to-hydration resume boundary as a `static
// const` binding the way a plain object/string/number does. Passing
// either by reference left the client silently reconstructing its own
// default (Gregorian calendar, wall-clock "today" focus) after hydration
// while SSR correctly rendered the pinned Persian date, which showed up
// as both a visible flash and a structural (day-count) SSR/hydration
// mismatch. Recomputing them inline, the same way
// calendar-booked-dates.marko's `isDateUnavailable=(date) => ...` does,
// makes each render (server AND client) independently produce the same
// value from the same fixed inputs, instead of relying on one instance
// resuming across the boundary.
<Calendar
  locale="fa-IR"
  createCalendar=(id) => createCalendarInstance(id)
  defaultFocusedValue=toCalendar(new CalendarDate(2026, 8, 15), createCalendarInstance("persian"))
  class="rounded-lg border"
/>
